[38;5;252m[1m### Problem 1: Missing OpenSSL linkage[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The RNG module ([38;5;10mrng.rs[0m / [38;5;10mlib.rs[0m) uses OpenSSL's AES-256-ECB via FFI ([38;5;10mEVP_CIPHER_CTX_new[0m, [38;5;10mEVP_EncryptInit_ex[0m, [38;5;10mEVP_EncryptUpdate[0m, [38;5;10mEVP_CIPHER_CTX_free[0m, [38;5;10mEVP_aes_256_ecb[0m), but the [38;5;10mCargo.toml[0m didn't specify linking against [38;5;10mlibcrypto[0m. This caused the shared library to fail to load at runtime with [38;5;10mundefined symbol: EVP_CIPHER_CTX_free[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mFix:[22m Created [38;5;10mbuild.rs[0m with:[0m[0m
[1mrust
[0m[38;5;10mfn main() {
    println!("cargo:rustc-link-lib=crypto");
}
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m### Problem 2: Missing [38;5;10mSPX_hash_message[0m export symbol[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The C code uses a namespace macro: [38;5;10m#define hash_message SPX_NAMESPACE(hash_message)[0m which expands to [38;5;10mSPX_hash_message[0m. The test runner ([38;5;10mrunner/src/main.rs[0m) looks for the symbol [38;5;10mSPX_hash_message[0m in the shared library. The Rust translation had the function as an internal [38;5;10mpub fn hash_message(...)[0m in [38;5;10mhash.rs[0m but never exported it as a C-compatible [38;5;10mSPX_hash_message[0m symbol.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mFix:[22m Added a [38;5;10m#[no_mangle] pub extern "C" fn SPX_hash_message(...)[0m wrapper in [38;5;10mlib.rs[0m that converts raw pointers to slices and delegates to the internal [38;5;10mhash::hash_message()[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
